.TH PCC 1 "October 2026" "PC Command Center 4.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pcc \- lightweight terminal dashboard for Linux system status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcc
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcc
is a Bash terminal dashboard for CPU, memory, filesystem, network, process,
temperature, and optional battery telemetry. It offers a live terminal display
and a one-shot snapshot mode.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SECONDS"
Set the refresh interval from 1 to 999999 seconds. The default is 5 seconds.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable terminal colors.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Print one dashboard snapshot and exit. Process CPU values in this mode are
lifetime averages reported by
.BR ps .
.TP
.BI \-\-layout " LAYOUT"
Force one of the
.BR compact ,
.BR mini ,
.BR micro ,
.B tiny,
or
.B wide
layouts regardless of terminal dimensions.
.TP
.B \-\-wide
Alias for
.BR "--layout wide" .
.TP
.BI \-\-disk " PATH"
Monitor the filesystem containing an existing directory. The default is
.BR / .
.TP
.BI \-\-watch-disk " PATH"
Add an extra filesystem selection to Storage. Repeat up to eight times. The first
command-line selection replaces configured WATCH_DISK entries; --disk is unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " NAME"
Select a kernel block device for read/write throughput, separately from capacity.
.TP
.BI \-\-view " VIEW"
Select overview, processes, storage, network, or pressure at startup.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
Select terminal (default), json, or csv. JSON and CSV imply one-shot output.
.TP
.BI \-\-record " PATH"
Record completed live samples in a new private CSV file, limited to 100 MiB.
Requires interactive stdin/stdout and cannot be combined with one-shot output.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Select a literal configuration profile. Last profile wins; all CLI flags override
profile settings regardless of argument order. A missing profile is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-list-profiles
List valid profile names and exit without collecting telemetry.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Print read-only dependency, configuration, terminal and telemetry diagnostics.
Does not start the TUI, sampling, recording, installation or automatic repair.
Cannot be combined with JSON/CSV output or recording.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.SH LIVE CONTROLS
In live mode, use the following keys:
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B r
Refresh immediately.
.TP
.B +
Increase the refresh rate, down to a one-second interval.
.TP
.B -
Decrease the refresh rate.
.TP
.B c, m
Sort processes by CPU or memory usage.
.TP
.B p
Pause or resume automatic refresh.
.TP
.B ?
Show help. Press any key to dismiss it.
.TP
.B n
Cycle through active network interfaces when more than one is available.
.TP
.B 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
Select Overview, Processes, Storage, Network, or Pressure.
.TP
.B e
Toggle the bounded recent-event list.
.TP
.B j, k
Move process selection or scroll storage/events.
.TP
.B /
Edit a literal, case-insensitive process name/PID filter (maximum 128 characters).
Enter applies, Escape cancels, and backspace edits. q is literal while editing.
.TP
.B Enter, Escape
Open selected process details or return to the process list.
.TP
.B a
Toggle full command arguments in process details. The next sample reads arguments;
r requests a sample immediately. Arguments are off by default.
.SH SAMPLING AND HISTORY
Resize, sorting, scrolling, view changes, and help dismissal repaint the last
sample without collecting another. History stores at most HISTORY_SAMPLES values
per series in session memory. Wide Overview and Network display ASCII histories;
smaller overview layouts retain numeric summaries. Each point is one sample, not a
fixed time bucket. The caption gives the actual elapsed span.
.PP
CPU/RAM use 0-100 percent scales. RX/TX share the displayed network peak.
The intensity alphabet is ._:-=+*#%@. A question mark denotes missing/warming data
and a vertical bar marks a sampling gap. A measured zero remains distinct.
.PP
Pause/help suspend automatic collection. Manual refresh remains available while
paused. Resume and long gaps reset rate baselines. Elapsed time uses /proc/uptime;
wall-clock timestamps are UTC. The first affected rate may be unavailable.
.SH WARNINGS
Threshold conditions must be observed for ALERT_TRIGGER_SECONDS before becoming
active. Recovery uses ALERT_RECOVERY_SECONDS and a lower recovery boundary.
The defaults are 30 seconds, 15 seconds, and a margin of five percentage points
(or five degrees Celsius for temperature). Load uses percent of online CPU capacity.
.PP
A gap exceeding twice the scheduled interval resets pending confirmation. Missing
data makes an active warning stale rather than reporting recovery. Repeated samples
do not duplicate transition events. Small headings show [!] for active warnings
and [REC!] for recording failures. One-shot output does not claim sustained history.
.SH INVESTIGATION
Process selection uses PID plus kernel start time and survives sorting without
transferring to a reused PID. Live CPU can exceed 100 percent on multiple cores;
one-shot AVG is the ps lifetime average. Details include user, state, elapsed age,
RSS, threads, parent PID, CPU and memory. Control/non-ASCII display bytes become
question marks. Processes cannot be modified or terminated through this interface.
.PP
Storage groups paths by filesystem device identity, including bind aliases. It
shows capacity/free space and inode usage separately from explicit block-device
throughput. Unavailable inode accounting shows N/A. Changed/unmounted selections
remain unavailable until restart. Remote, FUSE, and autofs mounts are not probed.
Local df is batched under a two-second timeout plus one-second termination grace.
These synchronous probes can delay input while running. Device rates use 512-byte
sectors and never sum overlapping disks, partitions, or mapped layers.
.PP
Pressure displays available CPU/memory/I/O stall percentages over 10, 60 and 300
seconds. System-level CPU full is undefined and omitted. Missing data shows N/A;
pressure does not require privileges and does not trigger automatic warnings.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The optional configuration file is
.IR ~/.config/pc-command-center.conf .
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is read from that directory instead. Defaults are overridden
by base configuration values, then a selected profile, then command-line options. Entries use literal
.BR KEY=value
syntax; shell expansion and command substitution are not performed.
.PP
Supported settings include
.BR REFRESH_INTERVAL ,
.BR ENABLE_COLOR ,
.BR DISK_PATH ,
.BR NETWORK_INTERFACE ,
.BR LAYOUT ,
and warning/critical threshold pairs for CPU, RAM, disk, temperature, load, and
swap. Threshold pairs are ignored when invalid.
.PP
Additional literal settings and defaults:
.TP
.B HISTORY_ENABLED=1, WARNINGS_ENABLED=1, PRESSURE_ENABLED=1
Boolean 0 or 1; these features can be disabled independently.
.TP
.B HISTORY_SAMPLES=60
Integer 10-600.
.TP
.B ALERT_TRIGGER_SECONDS=30, ALERT_RECOVERY_SECONDS=15
Integer 1-3600 seconds each.
.TP
.B ALERT_PERCENT_MARGIN=5, ALERT_TEMP_MARGIN=5
Integer 0-100 percentage points or 0-999 degrees Celsius respectively.
.TP
.B EVENT_CAPACITY=100
Integer 10-1000 retained session events.
.TP
.B BLOCK_DEVICE=
Explicit kernel device name; empty disables device throughput.
.TP
.B WATCH_DISK_1 through WATCH_DISK_8
One existing directory per key; defaults are unset. Values are literal paths.
.SH PROFILES
Profiles live in ~/.config/pc-command-center/profiles/NAME.conf, or beneath
XDG_CONFIG_HOME when set. Names have 1-64 ASCII letters/digits/underscore/hyphen,
starting with a letter or digit. Paths and expansions are not accepted. No profile
is selected automatically. Files must be readable regular files and use the same
literal keys/parser as the base config; they never execute commands or include
other profiles. Invalid threshold pairs retain inherited settings.
.PP
Precedence: defaults < base config < selected profile < CLI. Individual WATCH_DISK
keys override matching inherited entries; the first CLI --watch-disk replaces the
whole extra selection. Selection is resolved before loading files.
.PP
Editable daily, development and low-power examples are installed under
PREFIX/share/doc/pc-command-center/examples/profiles (Debian: /usr/share/doc/...).
Copy them to the user profiles directory before selecting them. Low-power reduces
monitoring overhead; battery savings are not guaranteed.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Doctor reports OK (available), INFO (expected condition), WARN (limited or optional)
and FAIL (missing required command). It checks runtime commands, effective config,
procfs, terminal, memory/process tools, filesystems/inodes, device counters, IPv4,
temperature, battery and optional pressure, with next steps for limited sources.
Command probes use a two-second timeout plus one-second termination grace. Multiple
slow probes can extend total diagnostic time. No privileged/configuration tools run.
.PP
Doctor exits 1 for missing required commands, otherwise 0. Optional missing hardware
and ignored config entries remain warnings. Throughput requires two samples, and
per-process permissions or device availability may change after diagnosis.
.SH RESPONSIVENESS
Cached repaint width checks use Bash builtins and fitted lines avoid extra command
substitutions. Sampling correctness and existing formats are preserved. Collection
remains synchronous; process scans and bounded filesystem probes can delay input.
The optional development script tests/latency.py measures collection, cached render,
handler and terminal key-to-frame latency. Host load and resource count affect timing.
.SH STRUCTURED OUTPUT
Schema version 1 contains schema_version, app_version, session_id, sample_sequence,
timestamp_utc, elapsed_ms, interval_ms, text_encoding, disk_path, interface, and
numeric metrics. cpu, ram, swap and disk are integer percentages; memory_used_bytes,
memory_total_bytes, swap_used_bytes, swap_total_bytes, disk_used_bytes and
disk_available_bytes are byte counts; rx/tx are bytes per second; temp is Celsius.
.PP
Each metric has a companion field named metric_state: valid, warming, unavailable,
or unsupported. Missing numeric values are JSON null or empty CSV fields. Text
uses reversible percent-bytes encoding: unsafe/non-ASCII bytes become %HH, including
percent signs, CSV/JSON delimiters, and leading spreadsheet formula characters.
Decode escapes as bytes before UTF-8 interpretation. Hostnames, user identity,
process arguments, multi-filesystem tables, and events are excluded from schema 1.
.SH RECORDING
Recording creates a new regular file with mode 0600. Existing files, symlinks,
directories and FIFOs are never replaced. The parent must exist and support hard
links: a private temporary inode is atomically linked to the chosen destination.
There is no append or overwrite mode. One completed sample writes one row;
repaints write none and pauses leave timestamp gaps.
.PP
Recording stops at 100 MiB or a write failure, leaves monitoring running, displays
a persistent error, and sets the eventual exit status to 1. Failure to start exits
before terminal setup. Descriptors close on quit/SIGINT/SIGTERM; durability after
power loss and a final row after SIGKILL are not guaranteed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on normal completion (or doctor without missing requirements); one for
argument/profile errors, missing required commands in doctor, failed recording startup, or
recording failure/size limit during the session.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Print a snapshot:
.B pcc --once
.TP
Use a three-second refresh interval without colors:
.B pcc --interval 3 --no-color
.TP
Show a wide layout:
.B pcc --wide
.TP
Monitor the filesystem containing /home:
.B pcc --disk /home
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pc-command-center.conf
Optional user configuration file.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The Debian package declares Bash, coreutils, grep, hostname, iproute2, procps,
sed, mawk or gawk, and ncurses-bin as dependencies. Temperature readings from
.B sensors
are optional and require
.BR lm-sensors .
.SH AUTHOR
PC Command Center contributors.
.SH SEE ALSO
